# .w7p5a-real-run-blocklist -- W7P5-a's real-run BLOCKER register.
#
# W7P5-a ("agent/PLAN-tooling-transformation.md") requires, for each of the 48
# deploy/release bash scripts under port: either a K=5 shadow-gate ledger
# asserting `equivalence holds`, or an allowlist entry with a BLOCKER. No
# third state.
#
# Every path below has its DRY-RUN-parity port as separate, reachable work --
# stubbed tool binaries make that achievable without touching production, and
# this file does not claim otherwise. Where it is done the evidence is named
# on the entry's own BLOCKER line, and for the Cloudflare/R2 family re-driven
# under the `w7p5a-` pair namespace on 2026-09-20 it is also a `dry_run_ledger`
# field on that path's `.ci/shadow/w7p5a-status.json` row. What each entry
# blocks is narrower: the box's "one real run each" clause, which for these
# paths means invoking a real external system (Cloudflare, R2/AWS, GitHub,
# ghcr.io, or a real `git push`) that this session has no credentials for and
# is forbidden from touching. `.ci/shadow/w7p5a-status.json` tracks the box's
# overall progress; this file is the checked, BLOCKER-gated record of why the
# paths listed below cannot get a real-run leg here. How many there are is the
# gate's line to print, not this header's to hard-code: it cross-checks this
# file against the status file's "blocked" set in both directions and reports
# the count it found. The figure stood at 39 here while the real set was 32.
#
# Liveness is enforced IN-GATE (the `.runner-advice-allowlist` /
# `.profiler-coverage-allowlist` precedent, `docs/agent-reference/suppressions.md`):
# the reader treats an entry as live while (a) the path still exists in the
# tree, (b) `.ci/shadow/w7p5a-status.json` still records that path as
# "blocked" rather than "ledger", and (c) the named external tool
# (wrangler / aws / gh / curl / docker buildx / git push) is still present in
# the script's real-run branch. A path that graduates to a K=5 ledger, or
# whose real-run branch drops the named tool, is stale here and must be
# removed by the person who did the work -- do not add it to the baseline
# by leaving it in place unfixed.
#
# A `dry_run_ledger` on a status row is NOT that graduation and does not make
# an entry stale. It records a comparison in which every external tool was a
# recording stub, so it is evidence about the port and none at all about the
# real run this file blocks. Clause (b) above reads `status`, which stays
# "blocked" for exactly that reason, and
# `.ci/rediacc_ci/tests/test_w7p5a_dry_run_ledgers.py` fails if the two are
# ever allowed to drift apart.

